            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The case concerns the Marietta Memorial Hospital Employee Health Benefit Plan (the \u201cMarietta Plan\u201d) and DaVita Inc. over whether the plan differentiates benefits for patients with end\u2011stage renal disease (ESRD) in violation of the Medicare Secondary Payer Act."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "27a67cc0da8009ca93fd46a3b69801429004ed193ea6870fd35b6664f0fc2aa4",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-27a67cc0da80-0000-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 9,
              "start": 9
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Supreme Court held that the Marietta Plan does not differentiate the benefits it provides to ESRD patients versus other enrollees."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "27a67cc0da8009ca93fd46a3b69801429004ed193ea6870fd35b6664f0fc2aa4",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-27a67cc0da80-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 13,
              "start": 13
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Kagan filed a dissent arguing that Congress did not intend to allow the plan\u2019s approach and that the statute should be fixed."
      }
    ],
